# GullyCoach — Validation Results

## Why I'm documenting this

When I first got MediaPipe working, seeing a skeleton move with my body was pretty exciting.

But I soon realized that just because the program displays an angle doesn't mean the angle is correct.

I decided to run a few experiments to understand how accurate and stable the measurements were, and where the tracking system was struggling.

This document contains the results from the initial development of GullyCoach, up to V0.1.5.

---

## Experiment 1 — Knee-angle calculation

**Objective:** Check whether GullyCoach can calculate reasonable knee angles from body landmarks.

### Method

I used a recorded video of myself performing a squat.

MediaPipe estimated the hip, knee, and ankle positions, and I calculated the angle at the knee using vector geometry.

The video contained approximately 293 frames at roughly 24 FPS.

### Results

The right-knee measurement changed from approximately 176° while standing to around 30° during the deepest part of the squat, before returning to approximately 174°.

The overall movement was captured, but landmark detection wasn't equally reliable for both legs.

| Measurement | Result |
|---|---:|
| Total frames | ~293 |
| Valid right-knee frames | 208 |
| Valid left-knee frames | 20 |
| Right-knee detection rate | ~71% |
| Left-knee detection rate | ~6.8% |

### What I learned

The system could capture the general bending movement, but some joints were much harder to track.

The camera angle and overlapping legs appeared to be important factors.

This experiment showed that getting a plausible angle is only one part of the problem. Reliable landmark detection matters just as much.

---

## Experiment 2 — Comparing automatic and manual measurements

**Objective:** Compare MediaPipe's knee-angle measurements with angles calculated using manually selected joint positions.

### Method

I selected five frames from the squat video.

For each frame, I manually marked the hip, knee, and ankle, then calculated a knee angle from those points.

I compared these angles with GullyCoach's right-knee measurements.

### Results

| Frame | Manual angle | GullyCoach angle | Absolute difference |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| 39 | 176.76° | 174.96° | 1.81° |
| 63 | 131.62° | 133.95° | 2.33° |
| 87 | 53.05° | 48.91° | 4.13° |
| 121 | 28.00° | 31.89° | 3.89° |
| 207 | 170.26° | 174.01° | 3.75° |

**Mean absolute difference: approximately 3.18°.**

### What I learned

The automatic measurements were reasonably close to my manually annotated 2D measurements in these five frames.

However, this was not a proper ground-truth accuracy study.

Manual annotation can introduce errors, and both methods measure angles projected onto a 2D image rather than actual anatomical joint angles.

Five selected frames are also far too few to establish general accuracy.

One interesting result was frame 121. The estimated knee position appeared noticeably displaced from my manually selected knee position, even though the calculated angles were relatively close.

That made me investigate whether MediaPipe was confusing the left and right knees.

---

## Experiment 3 — Investigating knee landmark stability

**Objective:** Investigate possible landmark jumps or left/right knee identity confusion during the squat.

### Method

I analyzed frames 105–140 and tracked both knee landmarks.

The diagnostic recorded:

- Knee coordinates
- Landmark visibility
- Movement between consecutive frames
- Suspicious movement flags
- Possible identity-swap warnings

### Results

| Measurement | Left knee | Right knee |
|---|---:|---:|
| Frames with usable knee position | 18/36 | 36/36 |
| Mean landmark visibility | ~0.61 | ~0.98 |
| Suspicious jump warnings | 0 | 0 |

The diagnostic produced no identity-swap warnings.

However, the left knee was below the visibility threshold for approximately half the frames.

### What I learned

The results did not confirm that MediaPipe was swapping the knees.

They also did not rule it out.

Because one knee was frequently unavailable, the diagnostic couldn't reliably compare both knee trajectories during the entire sequence.

I also learned that stable landmark movement doesn't necessarily mean a landmark is correctly positioned. An incorrectly placed landmark can remain in roughly the same wrong location for several frames.

