## Experiment 4 — Knee-angle smoothing

**Objective:** Reduce frame-to-frame fluctuations without losing the general motion of the knee.

### Method

I implemented exponential moving average smoothing:

**Smoothed angle = 0.3 × current angle + 0.7 × previous smoothed angle**

I recorded both raw and smoothed measurements during a webcam session.

The CSV contained 1,374 rows, covering approximately 47.6 seconds.

### Results

| Measurement | Raw | Smoothed | Reduction |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Left knee median frame change | 1.88° | 0.75° | ~60.0% |
| Right knee median frame change | 1.45° | 0.76° | ~47.4% |
| Left knee 95th-percentile change | 10.88° | 5.18° | ~52.4% |
| Right knee 95th-percentile change | 9.50° | 4.27° | ~55.1% |

These results compare changes between consecutive valid measurements.

### Tracking availability

| Measurement | Left knee | Right knee |
|---|---:|---:|
| Valid measurements | 1,092 | 704 |
| Detection rate | 79.5% | 51.2% |

### What I learned

The EMA filter reduced frame-to-frame changes, which made the displayed readings less jumpy.

However, the recorded session included actual movement. That means I can't interpret the entire reduction as an improvement in stationary measurement noise.

Smoothing also creates a delay between the raw and filtered angle. Large changes in body position can temporarily produce noticeable differences between the two readings.

I decided to keep the smoothing factor at 0.3 for the current prototype rather than claim it was fully optimized.

The main issue is still unreliable detection during some movements.

