## Experiment 5 — Stance-width measurement

**Status: Initial functionality test completed; numerical validation pending.**

### Objective

Estimate relative ankle separation while standing in different positions.

### Method

I calculated the distance between the left and right ankle landmarks and normalized it using the average estimated leg length in the image.

The program displays the ratio and draws a line between the ankles.

### Initial observations

The live measurement responded to changes in leg positioning.

Some example ratios visible in my test recording were approximately 0.36, 0.59, 0.08, and 0.21.

The ratio was unavailable when one or more required landmarks failed the visibility check.

### Limitations

The stance ratio changes with projected ankle separation, but it can also be affected by body orientation and camera perspective.

The values haven't yet been compared against controlled reference measurements.

For now, the program estimates relative stance width. It does not determine whether a cricket batting stance is technically good or bad.

### Next validation steps

I plan to measure stance width under more controlled conditions by keeping the camera fixed and testing several known foot separations.

I also want to investigate whether using foot landmarks instead of only ankles produces more useful batting measurements.

---

## Overall findings

So far, I've learned that building a functioning pose-tracking system is much easier than proving that its measurements are reliable.

The current prototype can detect body landmarks, calculate knee angles, smooth measurements, and estimate stance width.

The main problems still involve landmark visibility, overlapping limbs, camera perspective, and measurement validation.

My next goal is to make the system useful for actual cricket movements rather than just general standing and squatting.

These experiments are early development tests, not proof that GullyCoach is ready for real-world coaching.

I'll continue updating this document as I test more features and collect better evidence.
